#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "7fbb5683-c98c-5903-aad7-b2b729196def" :question/math-academy-id "e-10118" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{3}(x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $y\\gt 1$." :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2,\\; y \\gt 1\n$$\n\nand a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b43fd1a1622f406b7679d9f0f34552e046b9bc94b75a3504677f30b347d283.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq x, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{y}\\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(u, v)dudv &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(u + v)dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} + uv]_{u = 0}^{u = x}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + xv]dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{2}v}{2} + \\frac{xv^{2}}{2}]_{v = 0}^{v = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(x^{2} + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "77be8895-aa13-5dba-90ff-980840ec2f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9221" :question/problem "![](images/46/46508c4856bb4a5086f7b21c3bf65758a0f9157179c661e63ae42944bd252cb9.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\non our diagram. Let's also consider the region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/aa/aa4e587b8951f264d1f22392fd8fea7ae3397010376b82bbcd3ffab0d8dc50b8.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = P\\left(X< 2, Y< \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{5/2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{2} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{5/2}\\int_{- ∞}^{2}f(u, v)dudv &= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{u}vdudv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}u^{1/2}vdudv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}[\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}v]_{u = 0}^{u = 1}dv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}[\\frac{2}{5}u^{3/2}v]_{u = 0}^{u = 1}dv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\frac{2}{5}vdv \\\\\n&= [\\frac{v^{2}}{5}]_{2}^{5/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4} - \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "3d1ac1d5-1e37-57fc-8979-b727cb24fac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9222" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n$F(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad F(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad F(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad F(0,2) = 0.15$.\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nis the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5)$.\n2. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}$.\n3. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}$. This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le X \\le 5, 2 \\le Y \\le 5) &= P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 5)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]) \\\\\n&=-P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]) - P(X \\le 0, Y \\le 5)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]) \\\\\n&= + P(X \\le 0, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]) \\\\\n&= F(5, 5) - F(5, 2) - F(0, 5) + F(0, 2) \\\\\n&= 0.9 - 0.3 - 0.45 + 0.15 \\\\\n&= 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "bed3e5a1-ab6e-51ee-8924-0dec972d0f33" :question/math-academy-id "e-9838" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 1,\\; y > 4$." :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 1,\\; y >4$, and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8ad9c26a96468741d20e886a3c18c56e248e529e90fa5908b0b472efd01e75.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta = D\n$$\n\nSo our region $\\Delta$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{y}\\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(u, v)dudv &= ∬_{D}f(u, v)dudv = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9eb2d0-96bb-559d-b134-c96c4f67c9f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173915} {:answer/id #uuid "e7450f20-683f-5b29-9b91-ba327d9aa516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173916} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbf191a-72d9-501b-82e3-2f6735f697ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173917} {:answer/id #uuid "2e457fee-1f1f-5f5d-a4f4-5fb241ee3b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173918} {:answer/id #uuid "77d5ff7b-faf5-58c7-a6e7-ad993dda120c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173919}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6870a21-257d-5058-aa1a-18345227a06c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173914}] :question/id #uuid "2996061a-7fb9-5ed0-bf07-62fdec460468" :question/math-academy-id "q-111909" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nIf $F(x, y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{5})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8761a460-67af-5891-a904-d0939782ea8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174401} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d4516d-450b-5883-8c5a-7161fd35a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174402} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8270aa-7e49-540a-90df-23f01ae7bb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174403} {:answer/id #uuid "ef541986-52dc-5f53-9738-ce885957d53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174404} {:answer/id #uuid "749f8fc6-2978-5577-b15b-34a726bf4f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174405}] :answer-field/id #uuid "752a1883-1da9-5746-a3d2-ac250438a8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174400}] :question/id #uuid "7ec3b2b4-bdd6-5bb0-988c-5cf4e342d853" :question/math-academy-id "q-137970" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n$F(10, 9) = 0.7, F(9, 5) = 0.4, F(10, 5) = 0.2$.\nFind $P(X \\le 10, 5 \\le Y \\le 9)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7af3a78-2189-55f7-924b-87ae9c355a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218